  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Enhancing the Resilience and Self-Sufficiency of Drought-Affected Communities in Gedo Region: Somalia</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Somalia is facing severe and escalating challenges due to climate change, including recurrent droughts, floods, and extreme weather events. Mass displacement, destruction of infrastructure, food and water crises, and the loss of livelihoods are among the dire consequences. The majority of these displaced individuals have sought refuge in urban areas, creating congested settlements with poor sanitation and hygiene conditions. Through this project, the International Organization for Migration (IOM) is addressing these critical issues by enhancing community resilience, improving shelter solutions, and enhancing sanitation facilities for internally displaced populations, with a focus on the Ladan internally displaced person (IDP) site in Doolow, Gedo region. This project, aligned with the goals advocated by the Greta Thunberg Foundation, targets vulnerable IDPs impacted by drought in the Ladan IDP site. It involves constructing a comprehensive women&apos;s market within the site to promote economic activities and support women and families. Additionally, IOM is providing improved emergency shelter solutions to households, allowing them to incrementally upgrade their shelters. Lastly, the project is enhancing sanitation facilities, constructing shared Ventilated Improved Pit latrines with flood prevention features and disability-friendly setups. Through these interventions, the project enhances the resilience and self-sufficiency of drought-affected displaced populations in the Gedo region.</narrative>
  </description>
